# Unpoly docs

Unpoly is the missing application layer for HTML. Links and forms update fragments of the page instead of loading a new one, so everything around them keeps its state. The application stays on the server and responds with plain HTML.

This skill holds the documentation of Unpoly <%= version %>, the same text as https://unpoly.com, one Markdown file per page:

- `references/learn/`: guides that explain a topic with examples (start with `index.md`).
- `references/api/`: the API reference, one file per attribute, function, event, header and module (start with `index.md`).
- `references/changes/`: the release notes of every Unpoly version, and the upgrade guide.

## Finding things

Search first. The bundled script needs Python 3.8 or later and nothing else:

```
python3 scripts/search.py "<query>"
```

Pass several variations of what you are looking for in one call: synonyms, the attribute and the function that do the same thing, the plain-English task. Terms are combined with OR: a page matching any of them is found, and pages matching more of them rank higher, so more words only help:

```
python3 scripts/search.py "close overlay" "dismiss modal" up-dismiss up.layer.dismiss
```

This matters most when you search in plain words: when you describe a task rather than name a feature, don't know whether Unpoly supports something, or look for a guide you don't know exists. Then pack the query with every word for the idea, e.g. `"loading indicator" spinner progress busy feedback`, and search again with different words if the top results miss.

Words inside one quoted argument also count as a phrase: pages containing it word for word rank higher. A word with a leading `-` excludes every page that contains it: `overlay -modal`.

Exact names rank their own page first, in any spelling: `up.render`, `up.render()`, `[up-follow]`, `up-follow`, `up:link:follow`, `X-Up-Target`.

Each result is a `<result>` block with a `score` and a `path` relative to this skill, plus the page's `<title>` and a snippet around the match. Read the file at `path` for the whole page. The top 10 results are shown; pass `--limit 30` to see more. Release notes and deprecated features rank lower.

Without Python, grep the reference files for any of your terms, ranked by matches per file:

```
grep -rEic "up-dismiss|up.layer.dismiss|close overlay" references/ | grep -v ':0$' | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -n 20
```

Or walk the index below: it lists every guide chapter and every API module. A chapter's first page links to all pages of the chapter, and a module's page lists every feature of the module with a one-line summary.

## Conventions

- Every file starts with front matter: `url` is the page on unpoly.com, `area` its section. An API page also has `name`, its real identifier, and `visibility` when it is `deprecated` or `experimental`.
- File names are made safe for every file system and shell: lowercase, `$` spelled out, and the kind appended (`up.$compiler` is `up-dollar-compiler-function.md`, `up:link:follow` is `up-link-follow-event.md`, the class `up.Layer` is `up-layer-class.md`).
- The first heading is the page title, followed by its kind in parentheses: `# up.render([target], [options]) (JavaScript function)`.
- Fragment links point at the heading ending in `{#hash}`, e.g. `[label](up-render-function.md#options.target)` points at `#### [options.target] {#options.target}` in that file.
- Links to pages that are not part of this skill point to unpoly.com.
- Lines in `<nav>` blocks are navigation (links to related guides, a chapter's other pages), not content.

## Check the Unpoly version

This skill documents Unpoly <%= version %>. Features deprecated in older versions are still included and searchable, but the guides' examples show the current way of doing things. Check which version the app uses: `unpoly` in `package.json`, `unpoly-rails` in `Gemfile.lock`, a CDN URL in the layout, or `up.version` in the browser. If it's older, tell the user, and use the release notes in `references/changes/` to see what changed and how to update.

For questions these docs cannot answer, point the user to https://unpoly.com/support.

